@page "/overview"
@model Fido2Demo.OverviewModel
@{
    Layout = "_Layout.cshtml";
}

<section class="section">
    <div class="container">
        <div class="columns is-centered">
            <div class="column is-8">
                <h1 class="title is-1">FIDO2 .NET Library (WebAuthn / Passkeys)</h1>
                <div class="content">
                    <p>
                        A battle tested library for FIDO2, WebAuthn, and passkeys using .NET.
                    </p>
                    <p>Bringing passkeys to asp.net and .NET Core.</p>
                    <p>
                        Available on <a href="https://github.com/passwordless-lib/fido2-net-lib">GitHub</a> and <a
                            href="https://www.nuget.org/packages/Fido2">Nuget</a>.
                    </p>
                </div>

                <div class="notification is-link is-light">
                    <p>
                        The fastest way to see what this library does is the <a asp-page="/playground"><strong>Playground</strong></a>
                        &mdash; every WebAuthn Level 3 option this Relying Party can set, the exact request it sends, the decoded
                        response, and a Credentials tab showing exactly what a real passkey management UI should (grouped by
                        where the passkey lives, per <a href="https://www.passkeycentral.org/">passkeycentral.org</a>'s
                        guidance). The scenario pages below are narrower, scripted walkthroughs of specific flows.
                    </p>
                </div>

                <h2 class="title is-2">Purpose</h2>
                <div class="content">
                    <p>Enable passkey sign-in for all .NET apps (asp, core, native).</p>
                    <p>To provide a developer friendly and well tested .NET FIDO2 Server / WebAuthn relying party
                        library for the easy validation of registration (attestation) and authentication (assertion) of
                        FIDO2 / WebAuthn credentials &mdash; passkeys &mdash; in order to increase the adoption of the technology,
                        ultimately defeating phishing attacks.</p>
                    <p>Consider <a href="https://opencollective.com/passwordless">becoming a backer</a>.</p>
                </div>
                <h2 class="title is-2">What is a passkey?</h2>
                <div class="content">
                    <h3 class="subtitle is-6">The passwordless web is here</h3>
                    <p>
                        A passkey is a WebAuthn credential, branded for end users: FIDO Alliance's umbrella term for
                        the public/private key pairs this standard creates, whether they sync across a person's devices
                        via a credential manager (iCloud Keychain, Google Password Manager, 1Password, etc.) or live on
                        a single hardware security key. Either way, authentication happens without a shared secret ever
                        crossing the network, which is what makes phishing against a passkey fundamentally different
                        from phishing a password: there is no password to trick someone into typing into the wrong site.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        WebAuthn/FIDO2 is the open standard behind passkeys, supported by every major browser and
                        platform. It is not limited to web applications &mdash; support extends to native apps and
                        Active Directory as well.
                    </p>
                    <h4 class="title is-6">Read more</h4>
                    <ul>
                        <li><a href="https://fidoalliance.org/">FIDO Alliance</a> &mdash; the standards body behind FIDO2/WebAuthn and passkeys</li>
                        <li><a href="https://www.passkeycentral.org/">passkeycentral.org</a> &mdash; FIDO Alliance's passkey UX/design guidance</li>
                        <li><a href="https://www.yubico.com/">Yubico</a> &mdash; a security key manufacturer, with good general WebAuthn background</li>
                        <li><a href="https://webauthn.guide/">WebAuthn.guide</a> &mdash; a developer-focused introduction, from Duo Security</li>
                        <li><a href="https://webauthn.io/">WebAuthn.io</a> &mdash; a minimal demo/reference implementation</li>
                        <li><a href="https://github.com/herrjemand/awesome-webauthn">Awesome WebAuthn</a> &mdash; a curated list of WebAuthn/passkey tools, libraries, and resources</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>
                <h3 class="title is-3">Features</h3>
                <p class="help" style="margin-bottom: 0.75rem">
                    What the <a href="https://www.nuget.org/packages/Fido2">Fido2 NuGet package</a> itself provides.
                    Things this demo site does that aren't part of the library's own API are listed separately, below.
                </p>
                <ul>
                    <li>
                        <span class="icon has-text-success">
                            <i class="fas fa-check-square"></i>
                        </span>
                        Attestation API &amp; verification (Register and verify credentials/authenticators)
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <span class="icon has-text-success">
                            <i class="fas fa-check-square"></i>
                        </span>
                        Assertion API &amp; verification (Authenticate users)
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <span class="icon has-text-success">
                            <i class="fas fa-check-square"></i>
                        </span>
                        WebAuthn Level 3 server-side support: hints (&sect;5.8.8), conditional mediation
                        (&sect;5.1.3), the <code>prf</code> extension (&sect;10.1.4), and the &sect;5.1.10 signal
                        method payload types (<code>UnknownCredentialOptions</code>,
                        <code>AllAcceptedCredentialsOptions</code>, <code>CurrentUserDetailsOptions</code>)
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <span class="icon has-text-success">
                            <i class="fas fa-check-square"></i>
                        </span>
                        100% pass rate in FIDO Alliance conformance testing
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <span class="icon has-text-success">
                            <i class="fas fa-check-square"></i>
                        </span>
                        Security keys aka roaming authenticators, like YubiKey and Feitian BioPass FIDO2
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <span class="icon has-text-success">
                            <i class="fas fa-check-square"></i>
                        </span>
                        Platform authenticators and synced passkeys, like Windows Hello, Android, and every major
                        credential manager
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <span class="icon has-text-success">
                            <i class="fas fa-check-square"></i>
                        </span>
                        Backwards compatibility with FIDO U2F authenticators
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <span class="icon has-text-success">
                            <i class="fas fa-check-square"></i>
                        </span>
                        All currently referenced cryptographic algorithms for FIDO2 Server, including the post-quantum
                        ML-DSA family (RFC 9964)
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <span class="icon has-text-success">
                            <i class="fas fa-check-square"></i>
                        </span>
                        All current attestation formats: "packed", "tpm", "android-key", "android-safetynet",
                        "fido-u2f", "apple", "apple-appattest", and "none"
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <span class="icon has-text-success">
                            <i class="fas fa-check-square"></i>
                        </span>
                        FIDO Metadata Service (MDS) v3.1.1 attestation &amp; revocation validation
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <span class="icon has-text-success">
                            <i class="fas fa-check-square"></i>
                        </span>
                        WebAuthn extensions
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <span class="icon has-text-success">
                            <i class="fas fa-check-square"></i>
                        </span>
                        Intellisense documentation
                    </li>
                </ul>

                <h3 class="title is-3" style="margin-top: 2rem">Also in this demo</h3>
                <p class="help" style="margin-bottom: 0.75rem">
                    These are things this Demo project does, on top of the library, or that come from the browser
                    directly &mdash; not part of the Fido2 package's own API surface. Look at the
                    <a href="https://github.com/passwordless-lib/fido2-net-lib/tree/main/Demo">Demo project's source</a>
                    if you're building your own.
                </p>
                <ul>
                    <li>
                        <span class="icon has-text-info">
                            <i class="fas fa-flask"></i>
                        </span>
                        The interactive <a asp-page="/playground">Playground</a>: request/response preview, a
                        binary decoder, and a Credentials tab
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <span class="icon has-text-info">
                            <i class="fas fa-flask"></i>
                        </span>
                        Authenticator icons and certification status shown in the Playground &mdash; the library
                        returns this MDS data (<code>MetadataStatement.Icon</code>, status reports); rendering it
                        as icons and a hover tooltip is this demo's own UI
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <span class="icon has-text-info">
                            <i class="fas fa-flask"></i>
                        </span>
                        Grouping the Credentials tab into "Passkeys on your devices" / "Passkeys on security keys",
                        per <a href="https://www.passkeycentral.org/">passkeycentral.org</a>'s management-UI guidance
                        &mdash; again, this demo's presentation of data the library already returns
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <span class="icon has-text-info">
                            <i class="fas fa-flask"></i>
                        </span>
                        &sect;5.1.7 <code>getClientCapabilities()</code> and the actual &sect;5.1.10
                        <code>PublicKeyCredential.signalX()</code> calls: both are browser APIs the demo's
                        JavaScript calls directly. The .NET library's part is producing the signal payload (see the
                        Features list above); it never talks to the browser's credential store itself
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <span class="icon has-text-info">
                            <i class="fas fa-flask"></i>
                        </span>
                        The scenario pages (<a asp-page="/usernameless">Passkey Sign-In</a>,
                        <a asp-page="/passwordless">Passkey With a Username</a>) &mdash; example code, not
                        something the library provides for you
                    </li>
                </ul>

                <div class="content">
                    <h3 class="title is-3">Concepts</h3>
                    <p>The following section explains some of the concepts mentioned in the scenarios below and in the Playground.</p>

                    <h4 class="title is-4">Passkey</h4>
                    <p>The end-user-facing name for a WebAuthn credential. Per
                        <a href="https://www.passkeycentral.org/">passkeycentral.org</a>'s guidance, a passkey lives
                        either "on your devices" (synced to a credential manager, indicated by the WebAuthn Backup
                        Eligible flag) or "on a security key" (device-bound hardware). The Playground's Credentials
                        tab groups a signed-in user's passkeys the same way.</p>

                    <h4 class="title is-4">Fido2 vs WebAuthn?</h4>
                    <p>Fido2 is the umbrella term and branding of two W3C/FIDO standards: WebAuthn and CTAP2. WebAuthn is
                        the JS API that lets the browser talk to the operating system to generate assertions, and CTAP2
                        is the protocol that lets the operating system talk to external authenticators (security keys,
                        etc).
                    </p>

                    <h4 class="title is-4">Relying Party (RP)</h4>
                    <p>The Relying Party - often called RP - is the server that the browser communicates with. If you
                        are a developer reading this, the Relying Party is probably your webserver. It's the server that
                        runs the fido2-net-lib library.</p>

                    <h4 class="title is-4">User Verification</h4>
                    <p>A FIDO2 server (a.k.a the Relying Party, RP) can ask the authenticator to verify the user. This
                        can be done either via PIN code, biometrics or other factors that securely verifies that it's
                        the expected human in front of the device, not just any human.</p>

                    <h4 class="title is-4">Resident Credentials (RK) / Discoverable Credentials</h4>
                    <p>A resident (discoverable) credential is one the authenticator can find on its own, given just the
                        RP ID, without the Relying Party first supplying a list of candidate credential IDs. This is what
                        makes a truly usernameless passkey sign-in possible: the authenticator (or the user's credential
                        manager) locates all matching discoverable credentials, and the user picks one.</p>

                    <h4 class="title is-4">Backup Eligible / Backup State</h4>
                    <p>Two flags a WebAuthn credential carries: whether it is <em>eligible</em> to be backed up/synced
                        (a property of the authenticator, fixed at creation), and whether it <em>is currently</em>
                        backed up. Together they're how a Relying Party can tell a synced passkey from a device-bound
                        one, without asking the user &mdash; see the Credentials tab in the Playground for this in action.</p>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>
